diff --git a/docs/engineering/paperdiy.md b/docs/engineering/paperdiy.md
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/engineering/paperdiy.md
@@ -0,0 +1,763 @@
+# M5Stack PaperDIY experimental port
+
+Status: experimental. Earlier CrossMux candidates passed boot, SD, reading,
+buttons and the corrected Apps entry on this user's unit. Run 39285324943's
+conservative 30-update UI cadence passed the user's Home/Settings menu retest:
+smooth operation and periodic cleaning clears residue. Local/idle-clean trials
+were rejected. Sleep current and longer-term acceptance remain unmeasured.
+
+## Hardware and route
+
+The M5Stack K159 PaperDIY uses an ESP32-S3 N16R8, 16MB DIO Flash, 8MB OPI PSRAM,
+and a parallel ED047TC1 960x540 panel. SD is SPI SCLK39/MISO40/MOSI38/CS47.
+Buttons A/B are GPIO4/GPIO3. M5PM1 is at I2C 0x6e on SDA41/SCL42; PYG2 supplies
+the panel and PYG3 is active-low charge status. There is no touch, frontlight,
+RTC, IMU or audio in this profile. See the
+[vendor pin map](https://docs.m5stack.com/en/core/PaperDIY_Assembly_Kit).
+
+CrossMux base: `a78a39ed04d6755c6885d57521704d2f96f8b955`.
+SDK base: `9aa7e95e02c6374a23a562c883eb302552ff24f1` from `0x1abin/freeink-sdk`.
+PaperDIY changes are adapted from `prateek/freeink-sdk` commit
+`5663993f2c69a28efaddaefb7e4bf9e9899ef229` and `prateek/crosspoint-reader`
+commit `268216cdc3f5e7c5a016f476f57fa26d75047ebb`.
+
+Keep the configured SDK gitlink: the working SDK contains a local patch, not an
+integrated SDK release. Reproduction requires both firmware and SDK patches;
+an ordinary recursive clone of this base does not include PaperDIY support.
+The port adds a board profile, PMIC/button/battery wiring and display config
+selection. It retains CrossMux's existing display driver, renderer, applications,
+storage and BLE interfaces. Existing Board enum values are preserved.
+
+## Build and checks
+
+After applying both recorded patches to their bases:
+
+```sh
+python -m pip install pioarduino==6.2.0
+python scripts/patch_pioarduino_cache.py --prepare-platform
+pio run -e paperdiy
+sh freeink-sdk/libs/hardware/InputManager/test/host/run_paperdiy.sh
+python test/test_paperdiy_board_tag.py
+python test/test_hal_power_sources.py
+./bin/ci-check
+```
+
+The target uses the prebuilt `dio_opi` core, existing S3 PSRAM BLE configuration,
+and application LTO. The pinned M5GFX commit is
+`d8074bbac8b75d22149d128d71d55b6a34dbc808`. Outputs are
+`.pio/build/paperdiy/firmware.bin` (application) and `firmware.factory.bin`
+(merged installation). Record exact candidate build/test results, hashes and
+physical acceptance separately in the handoff report.
+
+The board tag is `paperdiy`; tagged images for other boards are rejected by the
+existing scanner. This target is not enrolled in release/OTA indexes. Do not
+use another target's OTA binary or assume an SDK-only PaperS3 profile is usable.
+
+## Input and power contract
+
+- Tap A: Up / previous page; hold A: Back.
+- Tap B: Down / next page; hold B: Confirm / menu.
+- WeRead tabs (new candidate, physical retest pending): from the first shelf
+  book, tap A once to focus the tabs. Tap A/B to switch Shelf/Manage, then hold
+  B to enter the selected tab. In Manage, tap A/B moves through actions; tap A
+  at the first action returns to the tabs. Holding A keeps its existing Back
+  action. Short B must not also enter content while it switches tabs. This
+  PaperDIY-only route preserves other targets' Left/Right tabs and Down entry.
+- Hold both: logical Power, using the existing two-button classifier.
+- Keyboard (PaperDIY only): tap A selects the next key in the same row and
+  wraps from the last key to the first; it never changes row. Tap B keeps the
+  column index and moves to the next row that has that column, skipping shorter
+  rows and wrapping to the top; if no other row has it, the selection stays.
+  Holds are unchanged: A cancels, B types the selected key. The side hints show
+  a right chevron for A and a down chevron for B.
+- The PMIC click is a one-shot logical Power event. Default Ignore retains
+  shutdown behavior; explicit alternate short-power bindings still apply.
+- Shutdown requests M5PM1 power-off. If USB retains power, turn off the panel
+  rail, wait for A/B release, then arm either key as a deep-sleep wake source.
+- USB cold boots stay awake. External supply detection is distinct from charge
+  status, including when the battery is full.
+
+No new framebuffer or dynamic allocation is introduced by the board plumbing.
+Its 64,800-byte facade framebuffer is in PSRAM; M5GFX, fonts and BLE have their
+own allocations. Actual internal/PSRAM heap peaks must be measured on-device.
+
+## Physical acceptance still pending
+
+Preserve verified original Flash backups and obtain approval for this specific
+new image before flashing. Match COM identity, image hash and offsets first.
+Back up SD settings/progress before testing a different reader fork.
+
+Validate boot identity, 8MB runtime PSRAM, SD mount and visible panel output;
+all four orientations, text/grayscale refresh and input during refresh;
+A/B short/held gestures, popup release barriers and sleep/wake on battery/USB;
+keyboard tap-A row cycle, tap-B column cycle and side-hint arrow direction;
+EPUB/TXT opening, cross-chapter turns and progress after restart. Test Apps
+individually: two physical keys do not establish that every game's controls
+are usable. Measure current before making battery-life claims. CrossMux-specific
+Apps, standby faces and BLE acceptance are separate from donor reader tests.
+
+## Initial physical findings and next candidate
+
+The first candidate passed boot, SD/fonts, EPUB page turns and reader menu checks
+on this unit. Home Apps selection exposed a shared home-menu bug with installed
+plugins and no OPDS servers: rendering omitted the plugin slot while activation
+included it. Rendering now uses the same hasLibrarySlot() condition.
+
+The panel photo showed old reading text on the home background, while a serial
+framebuffer capture had clean white pixels there. Manual forced refresh visibly
+cleared the residue. Repeated home-menu fast updates also accumulated ghosting.
+The reader-exit one-shot clean remains. Earlier four/eight-update periodic
+trials were too frequent; the later local/idle-clean trials also failed physical
+acceptance after repeated Home and Settings navigation. On 2026-10-20 the user
+requested the conservative 30-update cadence instead.
+
+## Current normal-UI refresh policy: 30 updates
+
+PaperDIY restores the pre-region LGFX implementation: complete-frame FAST pushes
+and the original Half/Full clean bank. There is no changed-region planner, dirty
+rectangle history, 1200-ms UI-maintenance timer or per-input cleanup cancellation.
+The ActivityManager render loop is restored to its upstream version; existing
+activity idle work (such as EPUB cache preparation) keeps its original behavior.
+Waveforms, voltage, rail sequencing and reading/grayscale implementation remain
+unchanged. Whole-frame submission does not mean every pixel is driven in FAST;
+Panel_EPD retains its own per-pixel comparison.
+
+The PaperDIY-only HAL budget is shared by displayBuffer(), displayBufferAsync()
+and refreshDisplay(): 29 normal-context FAST requests, then one whole-screen
+HALF request on the 30th. The counter measures completed refresh requests, not
+physical key presses, so status updates and explicit transition cleans affect
+its visible cadence. A committed explicit Half/Full resets the budget; a failed
+commit does not consume or reset it. Reading, text-AA and image contexts retain
+the requested mode and their own refresh cadence. Other boards are unchanged.
+The LGFX path is synchronous, so the PaperDIY async entrypoint delegates to the
+same budgeted synchronous path rather than creating another counter.
+
+The budget adds one byte of static state and no allocation. There is no second
+SDK counter or delayed UI cleanup. HAL promotion logs
+`PaperDIY UI clean: 30-update cadence`. Existing manual refresh behavior is kept;
+its physical recovery quality is not assumed proven by this rollback.
+
+Host coverage compiles the complete production HAL and LGFX driver, exercising
+three mixed-entrypoint UI cycles, failed-clean retry, explicit clean reset,
+reader-context exclusion, no pending driver maintenance, grayscale bank mapping
+and the non-PaperDIY path. Hardware acceptance must cover both Home and Settings
+across multiple 30-update cycles, pause without unexpected cleaning, manual
+refresh, reader exit and ordinary Apps/keyboard controls. Passing host tests does
+not establish optical quality. Obtain explicit approval for the exact new image
+before flashing.
+
+## Menu-navigation candidate, 2026-10-21
+
+This candidate adapts menu owners rather than remapping SDK gestures globally.
+The SDK still emits Up/Down for A/B taps and Back/Confirm for holds. Menu owners
+consume completed taps: A advances horizontally and B advances vertically.
+One-column lists only consume B; grids wrap within the current row/column and
+never select an absent cell. INX Recent uses a horizontal row in Flow, two
+columns in Grid, three in Icons and a vertical list in List/Cover. Apps and the
+library reuse their existing three-column icon geometry. INX main-tab A moves
+to the next tab; B or held-B Confirm enters content. Back returns to the tabs
+before the existing root/standby policy applies.
+
+Classic/Lyra/RoundedRaff vertical menus use B to cycle through the cover group
+and menu rows. Extended's adjacent covers use A within the cover group; B
+enters the menu and returns to the remembered cover after the final menu item.
+Carousel uses A within either horizontal band and B between cover/menu bands.
+Cover Grid uses A for its shortcut band and B for its cover selection.
+
+Settings/Text Settings use A to cycle categories/tabs, B to walk the tab-band
+plus rows, and held B on the band enters the first row without changing a
+setting. The setting-owned OptionPopup callers opt into B-only vertical
+selection; the default policy for app-owned pickers is unchanged. Existing
+two-value confirmation, save/cancel, numeric sliders and custom setting editors
+retain their behavior. They are not all converted into the same axis model.
+
+On PaperDIY, a held Confirm opens recent books/statistics entries and browser
+items rather than being reinterpreted as a remove/edit modifier. Held Back in
+the browser goes up one level or cancels a file picker at root; it does not
+jump to root based on duration. Non-PaperDIY shortcuts remain. File edit/remove
+long-hold shortcuts were initially disabled to preserve normal opening; the
+file/settings follow-up below supplies a distinct extra-hold entry.
+
+No new heap allocation, task, framebuffer or per-Activity data member is added;
+the layout-column hook adds a virtual function entry on list classes. Host
+coverage compiles production methods for both PaperDIY and non-PaperDIY,
+checks release-only snapshots, empty data, partial grid rows, category entry,
+cancel/confirm barriers and held-Confirm conflicts. Keyboard, WeRead input,
+SDK gesture classification and the accepted 30-update display policy are
+unchanged. Optical and functional hardware acceptance is still pending for
+this candidate; software checks do not establish that every theme is usable.
+
+
+## Menu-navigation hardware feedback follow-up, 2026-10-21
+
+This section supersedes the earlier B-only single-column policy. A taps move
+up/previous in one-column owners and B taps down/next, including browser child
+directories, Apps lists, vertical Home menus, INX statistics and setting option
+pickers. Horizontal tabs and grids keep A as horizontal next. Extended uses A
+within its cover row and previous in its vertical menu; Carousel/Cover Grid
+retain their existing horizontal navigation. Footer hints follow the current
+owner and focus. SDK gesture classification and the 30-update refresh budget
+are unchanged, as are WeRead, the reader and the known keyboard column issue.
+
+Library owns custom input rather than inheriting the shared list adaptation.
+Its tab strip now uses A to cycle Recent/Title/Author, B to enter books, and
+Confirm to show a three-action menu (Search, Reverse sort, Refresh library).
+Within books, A/B move previous/next including a return to the tab strip at
+the boundary; Back retains staged search/group/tab exit. Degraded lists wrap
+within their books without an invisible tab band. Confirm opens a book and
+never runs the other boards' hold-to-delete/group/sort modifier. Other boards'
+custom Library behavior remains. The new menu reuses OptionPopup, with bounded
+on-demand copies of three translated labels and its existing callback; no new
+persistent Activity fields or allocation scheme are introduced.
+
+Sudoku, calculator and Gomoku direction controls remain a low-priority usability
+issue. They are retained, not claimed fixed by list navigation tests. Source
+checks and host tests do not establish theme-wide hardware acceptance; obtain
+specific approval before flashing a new candidate.
