+### Feed-start candidate following diagnostic 40531224379 (2026-10-28)
+
+Live cross-page evidence captured EMPTY_LINE_QUEUE (0x400), one scan start/done
+and 0/5 successful phases, followed by unknown-baseline recovery (78 raw clear
+scans plus 30 redraw phases). The user observed repeated blackouts. This does
+not establish the cause of the separate persistent-fade report.
+
+PaperDIY now gates each phase on BOTH producers' committed-row watermarks,
+not the allocation ordinal. A producer that exhausts its assignments publishes
+an end marker. Each producer processes increasing global row indices, so when
+both watermarks cross N-1, every row in the required prefix has been committed.
+The gate never blocks a producer; it is retried after each commit and on exit.
+The prefix is at least the initial two four-row bounce fills (or the entire
+short frame), and at most either queue's usable capacity. Insufficient queue
+capacity aborts without starting a scan and wakes the coordinator exactly once.
+
+Reuse the existing owner array: reset to invalid before notifications, publish
+the actual owner with release ordering AFTER payload commit, acquire in the
+consumer. Unpublished rows cause a reported failure rather than consuming a
+later row through a stale owner. An atomic claim allows only one starter per
+phase; a late producer never restarts an errored scan. Prestart abort still joins
+both producers before queue reset. Existing post-scan join/reset ordering stays.
+
+Three atomic integers add 12 bytes of renderer context on ESP32-S3; no new heap,
+task, framebuffer or ISR wait. No waveform, clock, STV/CKV API, recovery clear
+sequence, UI30 cadence or reader page-count change. Other devices retain the
+original path. The prior diagnostic guard-removal comparison describes the
+historical baseline only; it no longer applies to this behavioral candidate.
+
+Host tests replay delayed rows, hold/cropped/short pages, prestart abort, bounded
+capacity, a genuinely full producer queue with two threads, mid-scan fault and
+next-frame reuse. They reject removing the readiness gate. Streamed consumption
+is deliberately paced by readiness in the host fixture: this tests ownership,
+order and liveness, not whether real DMA can overtake production. Device logs
+must continue to check error/start/phase counts during cross-page and reading
+tests. The candidate is not hardware accepted until those observations exist.
+
+# Free-trace diagnostic candidate (2026-10-29)
+
+Based on 40628068556. `FREEINK_PAPERDIY_REFRESH_AUDIT` now records successful
+FAST and TextTurn frames too. This is an investigation build, not a display fix.
+Waveforms, LCD/feed timing, power sequencing, history commit logic and UI30
+policy are unchanged. Code with the audit guards removed must match the prior
+candidate; verify that invariant before publishing its build snapshot.
+
+`PD-INPUT` reports logical press/release/held bitmasks and an input sequence,
+never entered text. Button bits use `MappedInputManager::Button` order. These
+are non-consuming snapshot queries; long holds are inferred from edge timing.
+`PD-HOME` reports actual drawing scope, theme, focus and cover-loading/cache
+flags. `scope=Full` means reconstructing the software frame, not a panel Full.
+`PD-GFX` reports orientation, gray/strip/font-scanning and clip state after the
+blocking presentation. Activity/setting logs provide page and theme transitions.
+
+Each `PD-AUDIT submit id=...` opens a blocking HAL submission; SDK `seq=...`
+records inside that bracket describe its actual scans, and `submit-end id=...`
+closes it. Native 16-gray commits also emit their own SDK sequence but need not
+have a HAL displayBuffer bracket. Input can interleave on its own task; correlate
+by monotonic device timestamps and submit brackets, not by adjacent line alone.
+
+SDK records retain target pre/post hashes and add pre/post history hashes,
+changed-pixel count/bounding box, four target-band hashes, temperature, draw mode,
+whole-screen selection, inspection microseconds and draw microseconds. Bounds
+are inclusive in native panel coordinates; `-1,-1,-1,-1` means no difference.
+Bands partition native source columns into four equal spans, not UI menu bands;
+use logged orientation to interpret them. Unknown baselines mark the initial
+history as untrusted. Software equality/scan success is not optical success.
+
+No new heap, framebuffer, task or ISR output is added. HAL/Home/input counters
+are four static bytes each. SDK fields and its 640-byte output line are stack
+storage; its old line was 320 bytes. Hashes are read-only passes over existing
+buffers before/after synchronous drawing. These passes and serial output have
+real overhead: inspect_us excludes printing, draw_us measures the update call,
+HAL elapsed includes the bracket and SDK output. Actual timing impact remains
+to be measured on hardware; do not claim unchanged observed timing.
+
+The desktop journal defaults to continuous capture (`--seconds 0`), retaining
+raw bytes, UTC-tagged frame/submit events, disconnect gaps and operator marks.
+Create `<label>.mark` containing a unique description to mark an observation;
+create `<label>.stop` to end after recovery checks. These control local capture,
+not the device. Never stop merely because a pale frame was reported. First
+photograph/mark it; then record the next UI30, a separate manual power-key refresh
+if needed, and post-recovery operations. Explicitly report a lost connection or
+capture failure. Journal/diagnostic code cannot detect physical paleness itself.
+
diff --git a/lib/hal/PaperDiyRefreshBudget.h b/lib/hal/PaperDiyRefreshBudget.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/hal/PaperDiyRefreshBudget.h
@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <stdint.h>
+
+// PaperDIY normal-UI cadence: 29 FAST updates, then one whole-screen clean.
+// All UI entrypoints share this budget; reader contexts retain their own modes.
+// Counts completed refresh requests, not physical button presses. One byte, no allocation.
+class PaperDiyRefreshBudget {
+ public:
+  static constexpr uint8_t FAST_UI_LIMIT = 30;
+
+  bool needsClean(bool fast, bool normalContext) const {
+    return fast && normalContext && fastUiPaints >= FAST_UI_LIMIT - 1;
+  }
+
+  void completed(bool fast, bool normalContext, bool committed) {
+    if (!committed) return;
+    if (!fast) {
+      fastUiPaints = 0;
+    } else if (normalContext && fastUiPaints < FAST_UI_LIMIT) {
+      ++fastUiPaints;
+    }
+  }
+
+ private:
+  uint8_t fastUiPaints = 0;
+};
diff --git a/src/components/PaperDiyKeyboardNavigation.h b/src/components/PaperDiyKeyboardNavigation.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/src/components/PaperDiyKeyboardNavigation.h
@@ -0,0 +1,113 @@
+#pragma once
+
+#include <components/keyboard/keyboard.h>
+
+#include <algorithm>
+#include <cstdint>
+
+// Loop-task-only selection state. A wide key can receive several column paths;
+// remember the arriving path until the user moves horizontally or changes it.
+// Four bytes, no allocation, no render/hit geometry or SDK input changes.
+class PaperDiyKeyboardNavigation {
+ public:
+  void reset() { columns = 0; }
+
+  void nextRow(const freeink::ui::KeyboardLayout& layout, int& row, int& col) {
+    if (!layout.rows || layout.rowCount == 0) return;
+    const int count = columnCount(layout);
+    if (count == 0) return;
+    row = std::clamp(row, 0, static_cast<int>(layout.rowCount) - 1);
+    col = std::clamp(col, 0, std::max(0, static_cast<int>(layout.rows[row].count) - 1));
+    if (columns != count || lastRow != row || lastCol != col || keyForColumn(layout, row, column, count) != col) {
+      column = columnForKey(layout, row, col, count);
+    }
+    columns = static_cast<uint8_t>(count);
+    // Built-ins have no empty rows. A custom empty row has no selectable key;
+    // only those empty rows may be passed over, never an ordinary short row.
+    for (int step = 1; step <= layout.rowCount; ++step) {
+      const int next = (row + step) % layout.rowCount;
+      if (!layout.rows[next].keys || layout.rows[next].count == 0) continue;
+      row = next;
+      col = keyForColumn(layout, row, column, count);
+      lastRow = static_cast<uint8_t>(row);
+      lastCol = static_cast<uint8_t>(col);
+      return;
+    }
+  }
+
+ private:
+  uint8_t column = 0;
+  uint8_t columns = 0;
+  uint8_t lastRow = 0;
+  uint8_t lastCol = 0;
+
+  static int columnCount(const freeink::ui::KeyboardLayout& layout) {
+    int count = 0;
+    for (int row = 0; row < layout.rowCount; ++row) {
+      if (layout.rows[row].keys) count = std::max(count, static_cast<int>(layout.rows[row].count));
+    }
+    return count;
+  }
+
+  static bool hasPhotoColumnPaths(const freeink::ui::KeyboardLayout& layout) {
+    using namespace freeink::ui;
+    if (layout.rowCount != 5) return false;
+    for (int row = 0; row < 5; ++row) {
+      if (!layout.rows[row].keys) return false;
+    }
+    if (layout.rows[0].count != 10 || layout.rows[1].count != 10 || layout.rows[2].count != 9 ||
+        layout.rows[3].count != 9)
+      return false;
+    const auto& bottom = layout.rows[4];
+    if (bottom.count != 3 && bottom.count != 4) return false;
+    return (layout.rows[0].keys[0].value == '1' || layout.rows[0].keys[0].value == '!') &&
+           (layout.rows[1].keys[0].value == 'q' || layout.rows[1].keys[0].value == 'Q') &&
+           (layout.rows[2].keys[0].value == 'a' || layout.rows[2].keys[0].value == 'A') &&
+           layout.rows[3].keys[0].value == QWERTY_KEY_SHIFT && layout.rows[3].keys[8].value == QWERTY_KEY_BACKSPACE &&
+           bottom.keys[0].value == QWERTY_KEY_MODE && bottom.keys[bottom.count - 2].value == QWERTY_KEY_SPACE &&
+           bottom.keys[bottom.count - 1].value == QWERTY_KEY_ENTER &&
+           (bottom.count == 3 || bottom.keys[1].value == QWERTY_KEY_LANG);
+  }
+
+  static int keyForColumn(const freeink::ui::KeyboardLayout& layout, const int row, const int lane, const int count) {
+    using namespace freeink::ui;
+    const auto& target = layout.rows[row];
+    if (!target.keys || target.count == 0) return 0;
+    if (hasPhotoColumnPaths(layout)) {
+      // Explicit user-drawn paths, not nearest-center guesses: 9 and 0 merge
+      // at L/Delete/OK; 2..8 reach Space. A language key takes the second path
+      // when enabled. This extension is documented separately from the photo.
+      if (row < 4) return std::min(lane, static_cast<int>(target.count) - 1);
+      static constexpr uint8_t bottom[] = {0, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 2, 2};
+      static constexpr uint8_t bottomWithLanguage[] = {0, 1, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 2, 3, 3};
+      return target.count == 3 ? bottom[lane] : bottomWithLanguage[lane];
+    }
+
+    // Other scripts, symbols and URL panels have different shapes. Map a
+    // stable normalized column into the layout's declared width intervals,
+    // including insets and wide controls; outside insets clamp to an edge.
+    // This is a defined fallback, not a claim that their paths were user-tested.
+    uint32_t units = 2u * target.insetUnits;
+    for (int key = 0; key < target.count; ++key) units += std::max(1, static_cast<int>(target.keys[key].widthUnits));
+    const uint32_t position = static_cast<uint32_t>(2 * lane + 1) * units;
+    uint32_t edge = target.insetUnits;
+    for (int key = 0; key < target.count; ++key) {
+      edge += std::max(1, static_cast<int>(target.keys[key].widthUnits));
+      if (position < edge * static_cast<uint32_t>(2 * count)) return key;
+    }
+    return target.count - 1;
+  }
+
+  static uint8_t columnForKey(const freeink::ui::KeyboardLayout& layout, const int row, const int col,
+                              const int count) {
+    if (!layout.rows[row].keys || layout.rows[row].count == 0) return 0;
+    // Horizontal entry to a wide key starts its leftmost column path. Vertical
+    // entry keeps the arriving path, including when several paths merge.
+    for (int lane = 0; lane < count; ++lane) {
+      if (keyForColumn(layout, row, lane, count) == col) return static_cast<uint8_t>(lane);
+    }
+    return static_cast<uint8_t>(std::min(count - 1, col * count / layout.rows[row].count));
+  }
+};
+
+static_assert(sizeof(PaperDiyKeyboardNavigation) == 4);
diff --git a/test/fixtures/m5gfx_paperdiy_draw_pixels.h b/test/fixtures/m5gfx_paperdiy_draw_pixels.h
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/fixtures/m5gfx_paperdiy_draw_pixels.h
@@ -0,0 +1,89 @@
+/*----------------------------------------------------------------------------/
+  Lovyan GFX - Graphics library for embedded devices.
+
+Original Source:
+ https://github.com/lovyan03/LovyanGFX/
+
+Licence:
+ [FreeBSD](https://github.com/lovyan03/LovyanGFX/blob/master/license.txt)
+
+Author:
+ [lovyan03](https://twitter.com/lovyan03)
+
+Contributors:
+ [ciniml](https://github.com/ciniml)
+ [mongonta0716](https://github.com/mongonta0716)
+ [tobozo](https://github.com/tobozo)
+/----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/
+
+// Test fixture: unchanged _draw_pixels body from M5GFX d8074bbac8b75d22149d128d71d55b6a34dbc808
+// https://github.com/m5stack/M5GFX/blob/d8074bbac8b75d22149d128d71d55b6a34dbc808/src/lgfx/v1/platforms/esp32/Panel_EPD.cpp
+// clang-format off
+  void Panel_EPD::_draw_pixels(uint_fast16_t x, uint_fast16_t y, const grayscale_t* values, size_t len)
+  {
+    int ax = 1;
+    int ay = 0;
+    uint_fast8_t r = _internal_rotation;
+    if (r)
+    {
+      if (r & 1) { std::swap(x, y); std::swap(ax, ay); }
+      uint_fast8_t rb = 1 << r;
+      if (rb & 0b11000110) { x = _cfg.panel_width  - 1 - x; ax = -ax; } // case 1:2:6:7:
+      if (rb & 0b10011100) { y = _cfg.panel_height - 1 - y; ay = -ay; } // case 2:3:4:7:
+    }
+
+    const int w = _cfg.panel_width;
+    const bool fast = _epd_mode == epd_mode_t::epd_fast || _epd_mode == epd_mode_t::epd_fastest;
+
+    uint8_t* readbuf = (uint8_t*)alloca(len * sizeof(grayscale_t));
+    int16_t btbl[4];
+    int yy = (y&3)<<2; int xx = x&3;
+    for (int i = 0; i < 4; ++i) {
+      btbl[i] = Bayer[yy + xx];
+      xx = (xx + ax) & 0x03;
+      yy = (yy + (ay << 2)) & 0x0C;
+    }
+    if (fast) {
+      for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
+        int sum = values[i].get();
+        int b = btbl[i & 3];
+        readbuf[i] = (sum + (b << 4)) < 248 ? 0 : 0xF;
+      }
+    } else {
+      for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
+        int v = values[i].get();
+        int b = btbl[i & 3];
+        readbuf[i] = std::min(15, std::max(0, (v + b - 8) >> 4));
+      }
+    }
+    auto buf = _buf;
+
+    if (ax) {
+      buf += y * ((w + 1) >> 1);
+      uint_fast8_t shift = (x & 1) ? 0 : 4;
+      for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
+        uint_fast8_t value = readbuf[i] << shift;
+        buf[x >> 1] = (buf[x >> 1] & (0xF0 >> shift)) | value;
+        x += ax;
+        shift = 4 - shift;
+      }
+    } else {
+      int add =             (ay * ((w + 1) >> 1));
+      int idx = (x  >> 1) + ( y * ((w + 1) >> 1));
+      if (x & 1) {
+        for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
+          uint_fast8_t value = readbuf[i];
+          buf[idx] = (buf[idx] & 0xF0) | value;
+          idx += add;
+        }
+      } else {
+        for (size_t i = 0; i < len; ++i) {
+          uint_fast8_t value = readbuf[i];
+          buf[idx] = (buf[idx] & 0x0F) | (value << 4);
+          idx += add;
+        }
+      }
+    }
+  }
+
+
